@using dorks_and_dice_site.Models.Site
@using dorks_and_dice_site.Services.Site
@inject ISiteModeArchitectureSummaryService ArchitectureSummaryService
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Personal Multi-Mode Website";
    var architectureSummary = ArchitectureSummaryService.GetSummary();
    var header = new dorks_and_dice_site.Models.Resume.ResumeDetailHeaderViewModel
    {
        Title = "Personal Multi-Mode Website",
        Subtitle = "Custom ASP.NET Core MVC site mode architecture",
        MetaLine = "Personal portfolio and community site platform | 2026",
        LogoUrl = Url.Content("~/site-modes/professional/images/favicon.svg"),
        LogoAltText = "Kyle Barnett favicon",
        LogoLinkUrl = "https://kylebarnett.com/",
        LogoAriaLabel = "Kyle Barnett professional site",
        InfoItems = new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            ["Purpose"] = "One codebase serving multiple site identities",
            ["Stack"] = "ASP.NET Core MVC, Razor, Bootstrap, C#",
            ["Status"] = "Live production architecture",
            ["Repository"] = "View source on GitHub"
        },
        InfoItemLinks = new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            ["Repository"] = "https://github.com/LegendMaster03/dorks-and-dice-site"
        }
    };
}

@await Html.PartialAsync("~/Views/SiteModes/Professional/Resume/_ResumeDetailHeader.cshtml", header)

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Overview</h2>
<p>
    This website is a custom multi-mode web application designed so one maintained ASP.NET Core codebase can serve
    different site identities from different domains. The professional mode presents my resume, portfolio projects,
    articles, and generated resume assets, while a separate community mode provides a different public-facing front door.
</p>
<p>
    The goal is to avoid maintaining separate websites when the infrastructure, deployment process, and some content can
    overlap. Instead, the app determines the active site mode from the request host or local development tooling, then
    uses that mode to control routing, branding, page availability, article visibility, and static asset access.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Problem Being Solved</h2>
<p>
    The site started as a portfolio/resume site, but it also needed to support a separate community-facing identity and
    long-form articles. Splitting that into separate applications would have created duplicated deployment work,
    duplicated UI infrastructure, and extra maintenance for content that sometimes belongs in more than one context.
</p>
<p>
    The current architecture treats the site as a small platform. Domains select a site mode, modes own their pages and
    assets, and shared areas such as articles can apply mode-specific filtering without requiring separate codebases.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Mode Model</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Professional:</strong> owns the resume, portfolio projects, professional articles, and professional media assets.</li>
    <li><strong>Community:</strong> owns the community-facing homepage and future shared-group surfaces.</li>
    <li><strong>Development:</strong> local-only preview mode for inspecting shared routes, unlisted articles, and mode behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>Unassigned:</strong> bare-bones fallback mode for domains that are not explicitly mapped.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4" id="architecture-flow-title">Architecture Flow</h2>
<div class="architecture-flow" aria-labelledby="architecture-flow-title">
    <ol class="architecture-flow-main list-unstyled mb-0">
        <li class="architecture-flow-step">
            <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                <span class="architecture-flow-label">Request</span>
                <span class="architecture-flow-detail">A visitor asks for a page or asset.</span>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="architecture-flow-step">
            <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                <span class="architecture-flow-label">Resolve Mode</span>
                <span class="architecture-flow-detail">Host and local preview state select the active mode.</span>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="architecture-flow-step">
            <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                <span class="architecture-flow-label">Check Access</span>
                <span class="architecture-flow-detail">Ownership rules decide whether the surface is available.</span>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="architecture-flow-step architecture-flow-branch">
            <div class="architecture-flow-options" aria-label="Available rendering paths">
                <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                    <span class="architecture-flow-label">Shared Surface</span>
                    <span class="architecture-flow-detail">Articles and framework assets can serve multiple modes.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                    <span class="architecture-flow-label">Mode Surface</span>
                    <span class="architecture-flow-detail">Owned pages, branding, and assets stay scoped to their mode.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                    <span class="architecture-flow-label">Fallback Piece</span>
                    <span class="architecture-flow-detail">Known fallback components and assets cover missing pieces.</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="architecture-flow-step">
            <div class="architecture-flow-node">
                <span class="architecture-flow-label">Render Response</span>
                <span class="architecture-flow-detail">The app returns the selected page, asset, fallback, or 404.</span>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Live Mode Matrix</h2>
<p>
    This public summary is generated from the same site mode model that the application uses at runtime. It shows the
    architectural boundaries without exposing internal host lists, file paths, or exact route probes.
</p>
<div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Mode</th>
                <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
                <th scope="col">Homepage</th>
                <th scope="col">Presentation</th>
                <th scope="col">Isolation</th>
                <th scope="col">Articles</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var mode in architectureSummary.Modes)
            {
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@mode.Name</th>
                    <td>@mode.PublicIdentity</td>
                    <td>@mode.Homepage</td>
                    <td>Mode-specific branding and stylesheet resolution with shared fallback behavior.</td>
                    <td>@mode.RouteOwnership</td>
                    <td>@mode.ArticleBehavior</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Representative Access Rules</h2>
<p>
    These checks are summarized from representative route ownership probes. They show the boundary behavior without
    publishing exact internal paths.
</p>
<div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Surface</th>
                @foreach (var mode in architectureSummary.Modes)
                {
                    <th scope="col">@mode.Name</th>
                }
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var probe in architectureSummary.RouteProbes)
            {
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@probe.Purpose</th>
                    @foreach (var mode in architectureSummary.Modes)
                    {
                        var isAllowed = probe.AllowedByMode.TryGetValue(mode.SiteMode, out var allowed) && allowed;
                        <td>
                            <span class="badge @(isAllowed ? "text-bg-success" : "text-bg-secondary")">
                                @(isAllowed ? "Allowed" : "Blocked")
                            </span>
                        </td>
                    }
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Request Flow</h2>
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-3 g-3">
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Resolve Mode</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Middleware normalizes the host, resolves the active site mode, and stores that context for controllers and views.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Check Ownership</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Route ownership rules decide whether the requested surface belongs to the active mode or a shared area.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Handle Mismatch</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Real domains receive normal 404 behavior, while local development keeps invalid states visible with a warning.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Architecture Highlights</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Mode-aware middleware maps domains to Professional, Community, Development, or Unassigned behavior.</li>
    <li>Route ownership rules prevent one mode from accessing pages owned by another mode unless explicitly allowed.</li>
    <li>Per-mode branding modules keep header and footer behavior grouped by site identity with Unassigned fallback pieces.</li>
    <li>Article metadata supports listing status, mode visibility, professional filtering, and noindex behavior for unlisted drafts.</li>
    <li>Professional static assets are stored under mode-owned paths and blocked from unrelated domains.</li>
    <li>Local development preview tools allow mode switching and unlisted article review without exposing those controls publicly.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Content And Asset Ownership</h2>
<p>
    Views are organized by mode under <code>Views/SiteModes</code>, and static files follow the same ownership model under
    <code>wwwroot/site-modes</code>. Professional-only images, PDFs, article media, and generated resume files live under
    the Professional asset path and are blocked from Community and Unassigned domains.
</p>
<p>
    Shared framework assets such as Bootstrap, site-wide CSS, JavaScript, and robots files remain in shared root paths
    because every mode needs them. Favicons are resolved through the mode presentation layer, with the shared root icon
    available as the Unassigned fallback.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Article System</h2>
<p>
    Articles are handled as mode-aware shared content. Each article can define whether it is listed, which modes may see
    it, whether it should be treated as professional content, and what post date should appear in indexes. Unlisted
    articles stay available by direct URL for eligible modes, render <code>noindex, nofollow</code>, and can be previewed
    locally through the development ribbon.
</p>
<p>
    This supports the workflow I wanted for technical write-ups: publishable articles can be proofread privately, then
    listed later without changing the page route or duplicating content.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Development Preview Tools</h2>
<p>
    Local development hosts show a mode selector and an unlisted-article toggle. The selected mode is stored in a cookie
    and remains independent of normal page navigation, so reloads and back-button behavior do not accidentally change the
    preview context. The cookie endpoint is only honored on configured development hosts, preventing public users from
    enabling development-only behavior with query strings or forged links.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Implementation Examples</h2>
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-3">
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title"><code>SiteModeMiddleware</code></h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Resolves domains, development hosts, preview cookies, and route availability before MVC handles the request.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title"><code>SiteRouteOwnership</code></h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Defines shared paths, mode-owned routes, mode-owned assets, and fallback asset behavior.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title"><code>SiteModePresentationService</code></h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Provides per-mode title suffixes, default metadata, article copy, and filter policy.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Mode Components</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Groups branding and presentation pieces by mode, with Unassigned fallback components for missing parts.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Connection To XnGine Framework</h2>
<p>
    This project uses the same modular design habit that appears in my XnGine Framework work: shared infrastructure
    handles common behavior, while mode-specific or game-specific modules own the details that should not leak across
    boundaries. In XnGine, the split is shared engine tooling plus per-game modules. In this website, the split is shared
    web infrastructure plus per-mode routing, branding, content, and assets.
</p>
<p>
    The domains are different, but the engineering pattern is similar: define clear extension points, keep specialized
    behavior close to the module that owns it, and make fallback behavior explicit instead of accidental.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Engineering Tradeoffs</h2>
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-3">
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Explicit Setup</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">New modes require domain mapping, branding, route ownership, and presentation behavior instead of inheriting broad access automatically.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Shared Infrastructure</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">Shared systems such as articles stay centralized while mode eligibility is still enforced at catalog and route layers.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Future Extraction Path</h2>
<p>
    Keeping the modes in one codebase does not prevent a future split. Because pages, branding, content rules, and static
    assets are already grouped by mode, a growing mode can be extracted into a separate application with clearer migration
    boundaries than a fully blended site would provide.
</p>
<p>
    In that sense, the architecture is a middle ground: it avoids premature separation while still preserving the option
    to turn a mode into its own program later if traffic, deployment needs, or product direction justify it.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Outcome</h2>
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-3">
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Single Deployment</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">One deployable website can behave like multiple related sites without duplicating shared infrastructure.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6 card-title">Professional Value</h3>
                <p class="card-text mb-0">The project demonstrates request-level mode resolution, content ownership rules, modular Razor structure, build-time generation, and development preview workflow.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

